The error comes from the row-level trigger body querying the same table it is firing on:

```text
SELECT COUNT(*) INTO l_count FROM parcels WHERE route_id = :NEW.route_id;
```

Because the trigger is `BEFORE INSERT ... FOR EACH ROW`, Oracle marks `HARNESS_APP.PARCELS` as mutating for that row-level execution. A row-level trigger cannot query the triggering table, so this statement raises:

```text
ORA-04091: table HARNESS_APP.PARCELS is mutating, trigger/function may not see it
```

A safer redesign is to move the capacity check out of the per-row section and into a compound trigger’s `AFTER STATEMENT` section. That avoids the mutating-table restriction because the statement-level section runs after the row processing for the DML statement has completed.

Important caveat: this replacement prevents the mutating-table error and enforces the limit for a single DML statement, but it does not by itself make the rule safe against concurrent sessions inserting into the same `route_id`. For true cross-session enforcement, the application or a parent route-control object needs to serialize inserts per route, for example by locking a route row before inserting. That additional design is outside the supplied context because no parent route table or lock object is shown.

Complete replacement trigger:

```plsql
CREATE OR REPLACE TRIGGER parcel_limit
FOR INSERT ON parcels
COMPOUND TRIGGER

  TYPE t_route_ids IS TABLE OF parcels.route_id%TYPE INDEX BY PLS_INTEGER;
  l_route_ids t_route_ids;
  l_idx       PLS_INTEGER := 0;

  BEFORE EACH ROW IS
  BEGIN
    IF :NEW.route_id IS NOT NULL THEN
      l_idx := l_idx + 1;
      l_route_ids(l_idx) := :NEW.route_id;
    END IF;
  END BEFORE EACH ROW;

  AFTER STATEMENT IS
    l_count NUMBER;
  BEGIN
    FOR i IN 1 .. l_route_ids.COUNT LOOP
      SELECT COUNT(*)
        INTO l_count
        FROM parcels
       WHERE route_id = l_route_ids(i);

      IF l_count > 50 THEN
        RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(
          -20351,
          'route capacity exceeded for route_id ' || l_route_ids(i)
        );
      END IF;
    END LOOP;
  END AFTER STATEMENT;

END parcel_limit;
/
```

What changed:

- The trigger is now a compound trigger instead of `BEFORE INSERT ... FOR EACH ROW`.
- The per-row section only records the inserted `route_id` values.
- The capacity check is performed in `AFTER STATEMENT`, where querying `PARCELS` is allowed.
- The original error number `-20351` is kept, with a more specific message.
- `NULL` `route_id` values are skipped because the original predicate `route_id = :NEW.route_id` would not match them anyway.

This replacement has not been compiled or executed here.

